  "response": "Adjudication between competing verdicts of blameworthiness begins with the vocabulary in which the problem was first posed. Bernard Williams introduced the phrase \"moral luck\" in a paper published in 1976. Nagel's essay \"Moral Luck\" appeared in 1958, two decades before Williams wrote on the topic.\n\nThe dispute is then organized by a taxonomy and a governing principle. Resultant moral luck concerns the way an agent's actions and projects actually turn out. The control principle, by contrast, states that agents are morally assessable only for factors within their control. Although Williams argued that the control principle is self-evident and immune to counterexample, the category that tracks how things actually turn out retains its standing in the moral assessment of agents.\n\nTwo standards of wrongness divide the field. Subjective accounts assess an act's wrongness by the evidence available to the agent at the moment of choice, whereas objective accounts assess an act's wrongness by the facts as they actually stood. In legal settings a further standard operates: negligence requires a breach of a duty of reasonable care. Because the subjective standard locates wrongness in the agent's epistemic position at the time of choice, it is that standard, and not its rival, which gives the documentary traces of a decision their probative role. Contemporaneous records of a deliberation serve as evidence of the reasons available to the agent at the time precisely because hindsight bias leads evaluators to overestimate how predictable an outcome was.\n\nApplied to a concrete case, these standards generate determinate questions of fact. Either the clinician foresaw the risk of serious harm before prescribing the drug, or the clinician did not foresee the risk of serious harm before prescribing it; one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be. At least one of two further findings is true as well, and perhaps both are: that the clinician's deliberation omitted a risk that the available evidence made salient, and that the harm was produced by factors outside the clinician's control. Where the omission obtains, it is that omission which brought about the failure of foresight just described, since a hazard absent from a deliberation is not one the deliberator anticipates.\n\nThe adjudication, then, turns less on intuitions prompted by the severity of the outcome than on reconstructing the decision from the inside: what the agent was in a position to know, which duties of care structured the choice, and whether the pathway from the choice to the harm ran through the agent's reasoning or around it. Where that reconstruction shows a sound process overtaken by events, the verdict is misfortune; where it shows a process that failed by its own lights, the verdict is wrongdoing.",
